A||||23||Raydar||The Sad State of GM (reposted)||||1||
Z||000000||Raydar||07-14-2001||11:06 AM||||I am reposting this from the Yahoo Fiero Mailing List. This was NOT my post. Merely passing it along... <BR>_________________<P>Subject: RE: Trusting GM -- long<P>Please do not misconstrue what I am about<BR>to say as harsh, for I do not mean it to<BR>be personal.<P>BUT... there is very little about GM Powertrain<BR>that should command anybody's respect.  The<BR>rest of the industry, and all the industry<BR>watchers, either laugh at the fiasco called<BR>General Motors, or shake their head in sadness.<BR>After all, it IS sad when the world's once-greatest<BR>automaker takes on water and can't figure out <BR>how to turn on the bilge pumps.<P>Powertrain is easily one of the MOST telling<BR>areas of GM's complete incompetence.  The<BR>following is not in dispute among industry<BR>watchers:<P>-  They have NO coherent six cylinder strategy.<BR>-  They have NO coherent four cylinder strategy.<BR>-  They have NO coherent passenger car powertrain<BR>strategy.<P>What do they do well?  Pushrod V8's.  Oh, and they<BR>make the best domestic automatic trannies.  But<BR>the former are useful only in trucks, primarily.<P>IF GM had a coherent six cylinder strategy, you<BR>would see the new Shortstar engine in a lot<BR>of vehicles.  Why isn't it?  It didn't perform<BR>like they thought it would, either in economy<BR>or emissions.  That was a BIG &^%$ing mistake,<BR>from a company with a tradition of great engine<BR>technology, AND after the mega bucks poured into<BR>that engine's development.  The new 4.2L engine<BR>in the TrailBlazer is wonderful, but it won't<BR>fit into FWD cars.  <P>As well-refined as the pushrod sixes have become,<BR>they are NOT suitable competition for the market.<BR>Please note that I am not drawing conclusions<BR>about their effectiveness as engines; I am merely<BR>saying that in THIS market, for CARS, you need<BR>four valves per cylinder, and overhead cams.  You<BR>just do.<P>More evidence of no coherent six cylinder strategy<BR>comes from Saturn's SUV effort.  Guess what is<BR>going in that vehicle?  A complete HONDA drivetrain.<BR>Yessir!  Built right here in Ohio,  Honda's own 24 valve V6, AND<BR>Honda's own automatic transmission, AND Honda's<BR>own powertrain controller, will be dropped<BR>STRAIGHT INTO THE SATURN.  Why?  Because GM has no coherent six<BR>cylinder strategy, and Saturn knows that it needs<BR>a good six cylinder powertrain to sell this new<BR>SUV in an increasingly muddied SUV market.<P>And does ANYBODY think that GM's four cylinders<BR>are good?  It is a pure abomination that the 2.2L<BR>engine continues to be produced in the Cavalier<BR>and Sunfires. (It is an abomination that those<BR>platforms are entering their ninth year of<BR>service, completely unchanged; more evidence that<BR>GM has no clue what it's doing.)  <P>The Twin Cam motor is a joke.  It makes great power,<BR>and gobs of torque.  But it buzzes like a WWII<BR>fighter jet, making the most unrefined noise I<BR>have heard from a four valve four banger since<BR>before 1990.  And reliable?  NOPE.  Not compared<BR>to Honda or Toyota.<P>Then there's the Saturn four banger.  It was AWFUL,<BR>and I mean, BAD.  It rivaled the Duke for horrible<BR>noise, vibration, and harshness characteristics.<BR>For 1999, Saturn redesigned the motor to be smoother<BR>and quieter.  It is.  Saturn then called Car and<BR>Driver to test the new engine, and proudly proclaimed<BR>that they used the Honda fours as the NVH standard,<BR>and that they had met their target.  Car and Driver<BR>drove it, and basically said "You didn't get nearly<BR>as close as you think you did."  Ever driven a "new"<BR>Saturn?  Not even close.<P>And then there is this sticky issue that GM<BR>won't offer manual transmissions in most of<BR>their FWD cars.  Why?  Because the bean counters<BR>think that the single control strategy, single<BR>tooling, single assembly builds, and single<BR>development, will make them more money.  They are<BR>only half right.  It DOES reduce the cost of making<BR>an automobile.  But they LOSE on the sales side.<P>GM makes virtually NOTHING I want in an automobile.<BR>For the money a Grand Am costs, I would rather have<BR>a Mazda, or Ford Focus.  Now, if you would be<BR>so gracious as to offer me a Grand Am GT, two<BR>doors, with the 3400 SFI and a STICK???  OH YEAH!!<BR>I'd buy one in a heartbeat, and I know lots of<BR>other people who would too!  Imagine having<BR>205 ft-lbs of torque that YOU can lay to the<BR>pavement through a five speed!  I'll bet the <BR>Civic owners wouldn't be so cocky then, eh?<P>How about getting a Grand Prix GTP with a five<BR>speed?  Wouldn't THAT be sweet?  Heck, if GM<BR>would just build more F-bodies with sticks,<BR>they would sell more.  Ever gone to a dealer to<BR>test drive an F-body?  I have.  Oh, they'll<BR>let you test one, but not one with a manual --<BR>because there aren't any.  The local dealer<BR>said they get one about once every six weeks,<BR>and it is sold before it rolls off the truck.<P>I can, however, drive to my local Ford dealer,<BR>and immediately ink a deal on a Mustang, <BR>sitting on the lot, with a nice five speed on it.<P>GM isn't getting killed in the pony car because<BR>nobody wants pony cars.  They are getting killed<BR>because, in typical GM fashion, they won't build<BR>them the way the BUYERS want them.<P>No, GM Powertrain deserves little respect at all.<BR>Making great eight cylinders alone won't do it<BR>for me.  And the lack of a coherent six and<BR>four cylinder strategy is NOT caused by just the<BR>bean counters; it represents a fundamental problem<BR>at Powertrain itself.<P>GM cares deeply about its declining market share.<BR>That's why "brand management" was introduced.  I<BR>haven't seen a concept so ludicrous since the<BR>GM 4-6-8 motors in Cadillac 20 years ago.  Oh wait!<BR>GM Powertrain just announced they are brining<BR>it BACK!!!!  Oh NO!!!<P>GM's problem is onefold:  NO CAR GUYS.  That's it!<BR>There are no CAR GUYS running that organization.<BR>The new executives were selling mouthwash and<BR>deodorant before cars, so to them, cars are just<BR>a product which is differentiated not by how<BR>you design and build it, but by how you MARKET<BR>it.<P>Frankly, I fear for GM's long-term existence.  I<BR>foresee a day in which they sell only trucks.<BR>Here's a shocker for you:  *I* do not think it<BR>outside the bounds of reality that one day,<BR>you will see a GM-Honda merger of some sort.<P>That's another post, though, and not really<BR>Fiero-related (neither was this one, but hey,<BR>sometimes we have to comment on how much has<BR>changed since the Fiero was made).<BR>________________<P><BR>Raydar<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 07-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Formula88||07-14-2001||11:41 AM||||While I do like my 99 Grand Am with it's 3400SFI V6, it sure could use a 5-speed (or 6).  The 3400 is a nice engine, but nowhere near high tech.  The 3.4TDC could beat it by 30-35HP as delivered from the factory - and it was available with a 5-speed!  And I do have to agree with the F-body debacle.  It's a fine automobile, but the Mustangs are outselling it left and right.  Partly becaue of marketing, price, and the fact that the Muscrat is easier to get the way you want it.  I'd love to have a WS6 6-speed Trans Am, but at over $30K, that seems a bit much.  Not to mention the wait for a special order to get the 6-speed.  ||||reg||6
Z||000002||KissMySSFiero||07-14-2001||12:20 PM||||i do agree with a lot of that.  gm needs to make a manual more readily available on all of their cars and trucks.  i had a friend that had to buy a used chevy truck because he wanted a manual tranny.  well new ones all came with auto/v-8 or manual/v6.  he wanted a manual with the v-8.  nope.  <BR>another friends dad wanted the same thing but he wanted a new truck.  they said six months.  he said ill get a ford and take it home today.  i could probably go on forever with things like this,  but ill spare you.  <P>and another.  why not v-8 in the s-10?  could you imagine that?  Dodge has them.  go to look at a dakota.  you can get a 4-6-or-8.  ford offers a v-8 in the explorer.  chevy no.  i am a die hard chevy guy.  but they have no clue what the market wants.  you have to satisfy the needs of all the customers.  or they get a ford or dodge, or even worst a daewoo.<P><P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <P>88FORMULA<BR>      <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>       <P>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg">           <BR>"Kids in the backseat cause accidents,  accidents in the backseat caused me!!"||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrgone||07-14-2001||12:43 PM||||i totally agree but wasnt aware of how hardcore gm is going down until you opened my eyes.<P>i did know that 5speeds were rare in gms now though. there is 1 5 speed grand am in canada i heard.||||reg||1
Z||000004||DecadenceR||07-14-2001||01:14 PM||||From what my friend said, he went in to a GM dealer to look at getting a new Z28, with a manual trans. And they told him it was going to cost MORE for the manual, AND it was a special order!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Black88GT||07-14-2001||03:57 PM||||Who wrote that? Wasn't Ernest Hemingway thats for sure.  I agree with GM 4 cylinders but thats about it.<P>He contradicts himself quite a few times. He says no powertrain deserves respect, but then he comments that GM is good for pushrod V8s and auto tranny's?  And he compliments the new 4.2 V6 in the Trail Blazer? Make up your mind! <P>Why do cars need OHC?  He never gives a reason, just "they do".  Whats wrong with the 3800?  Its one of the most reliable engines ever, with performance to boot.<P>I assume by "powertrain" he means engine/transmission/suspension, maybe not suspension but you get the point.  How can the new 405 Hp Z06 not get a "little" respect from this joker.  Car and Driver recently tested the <I>base</I> model 'Vette against 3 German competitors (M roadster,SLK AMG, Boxster S)and guess who won?  Hmm, the 'Vette!  It had a 10 rating for engine while the others had 9's.  In the transmission category it was only surpassed by the BMW, in the handling department it had a 10, tied with the Porsche, while keeping a 9 in the ride category, also tied for first.  (Where's the Cobra-R for these tests?  No where because its a low production overpriced mudstain)  Imagine if they would have tested the new Z06 against these other cars, it wouldn't even be fair.  The only car cheaper in the testing was the BMW M roadster, only one cheaper then a Z06 too.  Guess what, the Vette uses a good old push rod V8.  It might not be a fancy DOHC variable valve timed german engineered marvel of internal combustion engines, but it gets the job done just as well if not better.  <P>I have a lot of respect for all these cars but to say that GM has no powertrain that deserves respect is ludicrous. ||||reg||13
Z||000006||Raydar||07-14-2001||08:42 PM||||"GM Powertrain", I believe, is the R&D arm of GM that designs the engines that go in GM cars.<BR>The 3800 is a great engine, nearly bulletproof, but it's pretty much stone-age technology. It has it's roots in an engine that first saw production in 1963! If you count the V-8 that it was derived from (two cylinders were removed from the middle), the technology is even older. The only thing hi-tech about it is the EFI and the supercharger.<BR>The "Shortstar" was originally supposed to replace the 3800. I wonder why it didn't. Maybe it didn't perform up to expectations.<BR>Sure the Z06 is fast. 400+ horsepower will make nearly <I>anything</I> fast. But look at how many C.I./liters it takes to do it.<BR>Honda is getting what? 240 HP from a 2 liter? in the S2000.<BR>With the exception of the Northstar, every really hi-tech engine that GM has produced has been questionable, at best. The 3.4 Twincam was an absolute beast, but it's expensive to maintain, and more fragile than the pushrod variation. (NOT to detract from anyone on the Forum who has a 3.4 TDC. I'm envious.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ) Did they keep it and perfect it? Hell no! They discontinued it!<BR>The LT5 in the ZR1? GM didn't even <I>build</I> that one. It was built by Mercury Marine. And it was still notoriously unreliable. The earlier units were supposedly eating their own internals. Camshafts, I think. GM can't even <I>farm it out</I> and get it right. <BR>Everybody else is doing OHC and DOHC, even in their grocery-getters. My friends have a Ford Contour with a 200 HP 24V V6. Why can't GM do that?<P>Please don't misunderstand me. I still like GM cars. A 3800 Pontiac GP is my first choice if I buy a new car. But they can (and probably need to) do better.<P><BR>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 07-14-2001).]||||reg||9
Z||000007||Formula88||07-14-2001||11:14 PM||||Yeah, GM is woefully lacking in the high tech department.  Still, they've been building the pushrod V8 long enough - they got it right.  It's the best V8 currently in production, IMO.  Sure the S2000 has 240HP, but you have to wind it up like an 8 day clock and it has NO torque.  <P>But for the average car, or smaller sporty car, they just don't have anyting.  The 3800SC is a great engine, but I'm a little dissapointed that they had to resort to supercharging to get the performance.  A properly developed 3.4TDC would beat that easily, perhaps with less off the line torque, but still - normally apsirated.  <P>It has a lot to do with image, too.  And that's a lot of GM's problem.  Their designs are rooted in the stone age.  When you buy a GM, you're buying a dinosaur.  The F-bodies are admirable performers, but think how much better they could be with the same size engine and a bit more high tech?  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||FieroKyle||07-14-2001||11:49 PM||||That is a load of **** . One thing is most everyone wants an automatic these days because hardly and body even knows how to drive a fricking stick shift. Two he compared a Grand Am to a Mustang. The Trans Am and Mustang is a much more reasonable comparison. The Trans Am is offered a 6-speed in the formula models. Another thing, who give a rats ass if the hondas is getting more horsepower per liter the Corvette Z-06 is still the fastest production car in the world. Second who in the hell in the right mind get a Ford Focus over a Grand Am just because its not offered in a stick. The Grand Am 3400 V-6 can put a hurting to a new Mustang V-6. Second, the Grand Prix GTP has 240 HP, yeah it would be great in a stick but its not, face it, live it, your still gonna haul ass in it. The 3800 motors are great. They can reach awesome amounts of horsepower. But for most of the public, 200 HP in Monte Carlo SS is plenty. And really if that isn't enough they do come in supercharged versions in the Grand Prix GTP or the Bonneville SSEI. Also GM owns part of Honda which might explain Honda motors in Saturns. And for $45,000, a Z-06 Vette can be yours and have the fastest production car in the world not to mention. Its hard to compare European cars to American cars to Jap cars. They all have their own thing going. The Americans try to do it with the V-8s and higher end of performance V-6s. European cars sort of have a power sedan with the Mercedes, Jags, and BMWs. Plus then the high performance Porshes. The Japs, well uh.......they try to look fast more than anything, like the new Celica and Eclipse. Both which don't impress me. Also they try to go after luxury buyers that look at Caddys and Lincolns with the Lexus. Otherwise most Jap cars are just commuter cars. In todays car age, the caviliers and Sunfires are just sporty commuter cars, like the 4-cyclinder Fiero. High gas mileage, somewhat adequate performance into a really nice cheap sporty car which appeals to the general public. With high gas prices, your gonna see the gas guzzling V-8s go down a bit. And if GM wanted to they could make a top of the line "Hi-Tech" engine. But they don't need to. They already have the best cars in many classes with plenty of power. We are talking about factory stock cars. Nothing custom with enormous amounts of power. Hell.....You can make anything go fast with a little money, any engine any car, but its for the cars that deserve it, not a Chevy Metro or a Ford Focus (even though they have now came out with a SVT model), or a Saturn. GM makes the best overall cars in the world, has done, is now, and still will.||||reg||4
Z||000009||FieroKyle||07-15-2001||12:00 AM||||I totally agree with BlackGT. For the money, the Z-06 Vette is best the best sports car in the world. It does get the job done. How does it feel when their "Hi-Tech" engines get their ass kicked by some apprently by a stupid American "Low-Tech" engine. I mean give me a break. Now how do all those cars feel having their ass kicked by a lower tech engine. I guess GM doesn't know anything and have their thumbs up their asses (&lt;----SARCASTICALY!!!!). Some of you are retards. Wake up, GM may not make the most sophisticated engines in the world but they are far from under-powered, and unreliable. Sure the toyaota engines go forever but the car body only last 3-8 years because they rust out or the wheels literally fall right off the damn thing. Man I rarely get mad at people in the forum but these kind of statements get me upset when people down there own american cars especially when the only they say is they have high tech engines with more HP per liter. Give me a break, is that all you have against them?||||reg||1
Z||000010||FieroKyle||07-15-2001||12:05 AM||||Another thing! HI-TECH doesn't mean BETTER!!!! IF it is SOOOOO great and getting SOOOOO many HPS per liter, WHY ARE THE AMERICANS STILL BEATING THEM UP????? Think about it. Hi-Tech is almost a scam. Stick to the numbers. Like that one guy said, the honda does get 240 HP in its little motor but no torque. Go by the numbers, not whether the engine is the top of the line technolgy. It doesn't have to be Hi-Tech to be fast or a great performer.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Black88GT||07-15-2001||12:29 AM||||Not to mention with the S2000, your not getting to much more N/A Hp out of that motor.  They tested a Mugen S2000 in C&D and it had 15 more N/A Hp, somewhere around 6 grand for 15 more Hp, equates to about 393 bucks for each Hp over stock.  (Mugen S2000 is 57 grand)<P>FieroKyle, I don't think the Z06 is the fastest production car but it sure is one of the top 3 or 4.  Also its 55k not 45k.  Still, its the best bang for your buck sports car on the market.||||reg||12
Z||000012||Dslice||07-15-2001||12:40 AM||||GM still builds cars?<BR>I would agree except WWII fighters had around 1200hp and sound bigger than anything I've heard. I think my moms sunfire sounds more like those little gas model airplains.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||fldevel||07-15-2001||12:45 AM||||&lt;ignorence here  .. uh . whats a bean counter  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000014||hyhy4u||07-15-2001||12:53 AM||||Ya know...i was gonna write my opionon about that statement..but i kindof think i shouldnt be that mean to another human..what a dick!||||reg||1
Z||000015||FieroKyle||07-15-2001||01:20 AM||||Thanks for the Z-06 price correction Black, =). Yeah thats another thing, those Hi-Tech motors can't crank all that much more out of them because they are already the best they can get. ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Uaana||07-15-2001||03:50 AM||||Geeze I don't even know what side to be on here. On one hand I know someone who just bought a new Ford F150 and had a front axle break after 5000 mi and another who found the rear seat of his ZX3 hadn't even been bolted down. Then I watch the GT series on Speedvision and watch BMW's and Acuras beat Corvetts and Fbod's regularly (unless there's lots of straights where they can let their muscle flex) As for GM, when was the last time they built a car I would really want, much less afford? God I wish they would bring over the opel/Vauxhall VX220, hell even in limited numbers like the MR2 Spyder. For around the same price I'd have to get one. Instead they jump on the SUV bandwagon and introduce even more of the damn things. Hell the new Pontiac model even has a complete Toyota powerplant/transmission setup.<BR>I guess what I'm saying is all I want is a car that gives decent performance and handling without breaking my bank account.. Guess I'm keeping my Fiero with it's 3.4, GM can't do it but I can.<BR>BTW someone remind me not to go on rants after I've had a few.. because this made no sense and I'm just as confused as ever as to the next CAR i should buy.. <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Uaana (edited 07-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||Galen||07-15-2001||07:01 AM||||as a wise old man once said to me, "To  those who believe what they hear without understanding it fully, talks too much..."<P>Dont know what it means much, but it sounds cool   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>edit: wise man also say.. 'Spell my saying right dork'.. mean ole wiseman<BR>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="purple">Ray</FONT> <FONT COLOR="gray">-</FONT> <FONT COLOR="purple">"It may be the opinion of the court, but its still wrong"</FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1"><A HREF="http://www.ezRpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>ezRpg Networks</A></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Galen (edited 07-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||87GT||07-15-2001||11:34 AM||||I definately agree that the Grand Prix GTP definately needs a 5-speed!!!  I drove one the other day and while it was awesome with an automatic, I wish it had had a 5-Speed.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Fformula88||07-15-2001||11:43 AM||||I don't think GM's problem is their powertrains as much as the cars they are in.  Their pushrod engines use old architecture, but are reliable and offer great amounts of torque.<P>As for their choice of cars... most are boring family cars.  Pontiac, their excitement division is more interested in selling ugly SUV's than making a sports car.  The F-bodies are too big IMO.  I testdrove a 98 Camaro and it felt like I was piloting a Lincoln.  It was fast, but overall not that much fun to drive.  I like the Grand Am's, but they are not really sports cars.  After that, they don't have much of interst.<P>However, I think their biggest problem is reliability.  The last couple GM cars my family has owned (92 Trans Sport, 94 Buick Century,97 Buick) have had a lot of problems.  Things like alternators, starters, waterpumps, heater cores all were going bad quickly.  The rotors on the 97 Century had their surface flake off before 20K miles!  Its all little stuff, but it adds up quickly.<P>------------------<BR>John  <BR>AOLIM: Fformula88<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fierosig2.JPG"> <BR>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000020||Toddster||07-15-2001||01:06 PM||||I don't know what this guy expects GM to do. Let's face facts here people, The reciprocating engine is over 100 years old and is dead technology. Whether you have a "plan" for OHC or OHV or DOHC, etc etc is pointless. Most reciprocating engines are reliable and that is why some have been in production for 50 years. Until a more efficient engine comes along that better converts some sort of fuel air mixture into rotational force then puoring hundreds of millions of dollars into continually reinventing this 1800's technology is kinda lame.<P>Fuel cells, hydrogen, orbital and rotary engines could all have brighter futures if ANY car make, not just GM, bothered to put some real money into the problem.<P>This guy is having a spaz attack because GM has decided not to invest fortunes into time tested engines that work just fine. Go figure.||||reg||1
Z||000021||FieroKyle||07-15-2001||07:11 PM||||Good point Toddster, why stop doing a good thing, or in this case, why stop making those good push rod V-8s or anyother good engine that rolls off the line at GM.||||reg||1
Z||000022||Pontiaddict||07-15-2001||09:48 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroKyle:<BR><B>... he compared a Grand Am to a Mustang. The Trans Am and Mustang is a much more reasonable comparison.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>If you really think about it, the Grand Prix is more of a comparison to the non Cobra mustangs. A Grand Prix GTP makes more hp than a SOHC 4.6 Mustang too.<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B> ... Also GM owns part of Honda which might explain Honda motors in Saturns. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Actually Honda is providing the drivetrains because they made a deal with GM and are getting Isuzu diesels in return.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000023||Will||07-15-2001||10:02 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Galen:<BR><B>as a wise old man once said to me, "To  those who believe what they hear without understanding it fully, talks too much..."<P>Dont know what it means much, but it sounds cool    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>edit: wise man also say.. 'Spell my saying right dork'.. mean ole wiseman</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I'm with him. Do those of you who bash GM for being "low tech" even know waht technology is in their engines?<BR>For instance: the LS6 stays closed loop at WOT, on a standard--not wide-band--O2 sensor. Nothing from Japan or Europe does that.<BR>Specific output (HP/Litre) is almost entirely a result of high RPM. You want high specific output? Spin the engine faster!<BR>The real deal for a quality engine design is specific torque. Spcific torque reflects the quality of the cylinder head design, the scavenge and fill of cylinders, the amount of work going into the synergistic working of intake and exhaust systems, how the flow properties of intake and exhaust tracts harmonize with engine geometry, and a multitude of other factors. Specific torque is a much better indicator of the quality of a engine's design than specific power.<P>Read this article: <A HREF="http://www.airflowresearch.com/Articles/A3-P1.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.airflowresearch.com/Articles/A3-P1.htm</A>    A bunch of reasonably knowledgible individuals threw together properly selected parts and matched the specific torque of the mighty F20C1 (S2000 engine). And you know what else? It used a 215 degree cam and breathed through a F****** Carbuerettor!<BR>The F20C1 puts out 75 ftlbs/litre.<BR>The LS6 puts down 70 ftlbs /litre, and has a LOT more litres.<BR>Where's the huge technology gap?<BR>Don't forget that the Corvette outdoes the S2000 by 2 MPG on the highway. Even with outdated pushrod technology.<P><BR>I'm not defending GM. I think their current lineup sucks. They need manual transmissions, they need RWD, they need leadership with vision and balls. Heck, I know who Jack Nasser (Ford) is, but I have no idea who's in charge of GM.<P>The only reason I hope FWD lives on is so that I can have more parts for my Fiero.||||reg||3
